+	/*
+	 * When to escalate to a PCI power cycle, which is the only recovery here
+	 * that drops the link.
+	 *
+	 * Counting beacon-miss recoveries is the wrong signal, at any threshold.
+	 * Beacon miss is detected on a timer, so a saturated machine -- a native
+	 * Chromium build at -j2 is enough -- produces them steadily: measured,
+	 * roughly one every two to four minutes for as long as the build runs.
+	 * Density does not separate the cases either, because those are spread
+	 * out too. Two earlier attempts at this both misfired: "two within thirty
+	 * seconds" fired within seconds of load starting, and "four spread over
+	 * at least thirty seconds" fired after forty-five minutes of it.
+	 *
+	 * The real difference is in whether the link survives. Under load every
+	 * one of these recovers and the vap stays in RUN -- forty-five minutes of
+	 * it, eleven resets, association and DHCP lease held throughout. The
+	 * condition this escalation exists for looks nothing like that: per the
+	 * note below it recurs *every time the interface reassociates*, meaning
